## Proof

We show that the genus of complex projective space $\mathbb{CP}^N$ equals $0$ for every $N \geq 1$. We verify this for both standard notions of genus (geometric and arithmetic), confirming the answer is yes regardless of convention.

**Setup.** $\mathbb{CP}^N$ is a smooth compact complex manifold of (complex) dimension $N$. Its cohomology is well known:
$$H^k(\mathbb{CP}^N;\mathbb{Z}) \cong \begin{cases} \mathbb{Z} & k = 0, 2, 4, \dots, 2N, \\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$

**Hodge numbers.** By Hodge theory, $H^{p,q}(\mathbb{CP}^N)$ contributes to $H^{p+q}(\mathbb{CP}^N;\mathbb{C})$. Since the only nonzero cohomology is in even degrees and each such group is one-dimensional, the Hodge decomposition forces
$$h^{p,q}(\mathbb{CP}^N) = \begin{cases} 1 & p = q,\ 0 \le p \le N, \\ 0 & p \neq q. \end{cases}$$
In particular $h^{N,0}(\mathbb{CP}^N) = 0$ for $N \ge 1$ (and $h^{0,0}=1$).

**Geometric genus.** The geometric genus is $p_g(X) = h^{N,0}(X) = \dim H^0(X, \Omega_X^N)$. From the Hodge numbers above,
$$p_g(\mathbb{CP}^N) = h^{N,0}(\mathbb{CP}^N) = 0 \quad \text{for all } N \ge 1.$$

**Arithmetic genus.** The arithmetic genus is $p_a(X) = \chi(\mathcal{O}_X) - 1$, where
$$\chi(\mathcal{O}_X) = \sum_{i=0}^{N} (-1)^i\, h^{0,i}(X).$$
For $\mathbb{CP}^N$, $h^{0,0} = 1$ and $h^{0,i} = 0$ for $i \ge 1$, so $\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{CP}^N}) = 1$, giving
$$p_a(\mathbb{CP}^N) = 1 - 1 = 0 \quad \text{for all } N \ge 1.$$

**The case $N=0$.** $\mathbb{CP}^0$ is a point, a zero-dimensional manifold. Its geometric genus $h^{0,0}$ is conventionally $1$ (a single point), but its arithmetic genus $\chi(\mathcal{O}) - 1 = 1 - 1 = 0$. For the standard genus notion of a positive-dimensional variety, $\mathbb{CP}^0$ is degenerate; in any case the arithmetic genus is $0$.

**Conclusion.** For every $N \ge 1$, both the geometric genus and the arithmetic genus of $\mathbb{CP}^N$ equal $0$. Hence the genus of $\mathbb{CP}^N$ is $0$ for all $N$.

